    <body>
      <sec id="article-25599.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Deep neck space abscesses represent serious infections occurring within the fascial planes of the neck, where critical structures such as major vessels, cranial nerves, the airway, and components of the gastrointestinal system are closely situated. The complex anatomy of the neck, combined with the potential for deep tissue extension, makes these abscesses difficult to detect solely by external examination or palpation. If not promptly diagnosed and managed, infection can rapidly spread, causing life-threatening complications including airway obstruction, neurovascular injury, mediastinitis, or osteomyelitis. Clinical presentation varies depending on the affected space and depth, often including pain, swelling, dysphagia, fever, and systemic signs of infection. Accurate assessment and timely intervention are essential to prevent morbidity and mortality associated with delayed or inadequate treatment.</p>
        <p>The educational activity enhances participant competence in recognizing, evaluating, and managing deep neck abscesses. Clinicians learn to interpret imaging studies effectively, select appropriate antibiotic therapy, and determine indications for surgical drainage. Instruction emphasizes the critical role of interprofessional collaboration among clinicians, radiologists, otolaryngologists, anesthesiologists, and nursing staff, facilitating coordinated care and shared decision-making. By integrating multidisciplinary expertise, clinicians can ensure timely intervention, monitor for complications, and provide comprehensive post-procedural care, ultimately improving patient safety, optimizing outcomes, and reducing the risk of recurrent or progressive infection.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate deep neck abscesses from cellulitis, lymphadenopathy, neoplasms, or vascular anomalies using a combination of clinical evaluation and imaging findings.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Screen high-risk individuals for early signs of deep neck abscesses, particularly those with recent head and neck infections or trauma.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement appropriate imaging techniques to localize and define deep neck abscesses accurately.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate&#x000a0;interprofessional team strategies for improving care coordination to enhance outcomes for patients affected by neck abscesses.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25599&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25599">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Infections of the deep neck spaces represent a potentially life-threatening clinical entity that can be challenging to diagnose, localize, and manage due to the complex anatomy and proximity of vital structures. The neck contains multiple fascial planes and potential spaces that communicate with the mediastinum and skull base, allowing infections to spread rapidly and unpredictably. These spaces encompass critical components of the airway, gastrointestinal tract, and major vascular and neural structures, making even small abscesses clinically significant. Because the affected tissues are often deep and not externally visible or palpable, diagnosis relies heavily on a high index of suspicion and appropriate imaging modalities such as contrast-enhanced computed tomography or magnetic resonance imaging. The source of infection determines not only the clinical presentation but also the microbiologic profile, route of spread, and potential complications, including airway obstruction, jugular vein thrombosis, mediastinitis, and septic embolization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Neck pain is the most common presenting symptom, but it carries a broad differential diagnosis that may delay definitive management. Odontogenic infections remain the leading etiology of deep neck abscesses; however, the specific teeth and fascial spaces involved markedly influence the clinical pattern, severity, and prognosis, as distinct anatomic pathways of spread dictate the course of disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r2">[2]</xref> Other significant sources include tonsillar or pharyngeal infections, salivary gland infections, cervical spine pathology, and, less commonly, cutaneous, branchial cleft, or traumatic origins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r4">[4]</xref> Understanding the fascial anatomy and interconnections of the neck spaces is essential for accurate localization, timely surgical drainage, and targeted antimicrobial therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r7">[7]</xref> Early multidisciplinary collaboration, particularly between surgeons, infectious disease specialists, radiologists, and anesthesiologists, is crucial for optimizing airway management, preventing complications, and improving patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The most common sources of deep neck infections in adults are dental and periodontal, but in children, the most common sources are tonsillitis and pharyngitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Other causes include oral surgical procedures, salivary gland infection, trauma to the oral cavity or pharynx, septic metastases from intravenous drug use, cervical lymphadenitis, branchial cleft anomalies, thyroglossal duct cyst, cervical osteomyelitis or discitis, and others. There may be no clear source in 20% to 50% of&#x000a0;deep&#x000a0;neck infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r9">[9]</xref> Presentations of any infectious etiology may be delayed or atypical in immunosuppressed patients, and opportunistic pathogens must also be considered in this population.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Deep neck infections are common. As they comprise many discrete entities, it is difficult to accurately estimate the number of deep neck space infections in a given population. Assuming the incidence in the United States is lower than in countries lacking immunizations or early medical intervention for superficial infections seems reasonable.&#x000a0;According to an Oxford University Press article, approximately 45,000 cases of deep neck infection occur annually in the United States.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Peritonsillar abscess is most common in people aged 20 to 40. Children are not often affected by a peritonsillar abscess, but can be if they are immunocompromised, and there is no gender predilection. Chronic tonsillitis or multiple rounds of oral antibiotics may predispose a person to the formation of a peritonsillar abscess. Parapharyngeal abscesses can develop in patients of any age, but are most common in children and adolescents; true retropharyngeal abscesses are most common in children younger than 5 and are potentially life-threatening.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r11">[11]</xref> Immunocompromised adults are also at increased risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Prevertebral space abscesses are neurosurgical emergencies, although infection can originate from an upper aerodigestive tract source, a traumatic or postsurgical source, or an extension of spinal osteomyelitis or discitis. These are most common in adults and may present with vague symptoms such as neck pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>There are multiple potential pathways of infection in the neck, depending on the initial site of infection. These include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lymphatic spread</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Infection may proceed from the primary site via lymphatic channels to the relevant draining lymph node basins. This can lead to suppuration of the lymph node, eventual extension of infection beyond the node's capsule, and abscess formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Hematogenous spread</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Septic emboli from a distant primary infection may be carried to tissues in the head and neck, where the seed develops into a phlegmon and, subsequently, an abscess. Classically described in intravenous drug use (IVDU) with endocarditis, this can occur from other infectious sources as well, and is not limited to IVDU.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;This is&#x000a0;one proposed mechanism of vertebral osteomyelitis, which can progress to a prevertebral abscess.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Primary head and neck infection can also be a source of septic emboli that can seed locally or distally, as in Lemierre syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r17">[17]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Direct extension</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Local inflammation or phlegmon&#x000a0;initially develops,&#x000a0;which may progress to a fulminant abscess with a purulent fluid collection. This infection then spreads preferentially along fascial planes. A lateral pharyngeal space infection can spread into the carotid sheath,&#x000a0;potentially leading to internal jugular vein thrombosis and subsequent&#x000a0;thromboembolic diseases, or into the masticator space, leading to trismus. These can also progress into the retropharyngeal space and the so-called "danger space," which can progress to mediastinitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r19">[19]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Neck abscesses can include peritonsillar, retropharyngeal, submandibular, buccal, parapharyngeal, and&#x000a0;odontogenic infections. The retropharyngeal, retroesophageal, and posterior mediastinal spaces are continuous pathways for the spread of disease.&#x000a0;Within the neck, 11 zones are created by planes of lesser and greater resistance between the fascial layers. These potential spaces can expand, communicate with one another, and permit infection to spread. The spaces include the parapharyngeal and lateral pharyngeal spaces, which lie superior to the hyoid bone. The skull base is the superior margin, and the medial boundary is the middle layer of the deep cervical fascia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Organisms implicated in deep neck infections include aerobes and anaerobes, with oral flora frequently predominating. Commonly cultured organisms include&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus, Bacteroides, Staphylococcus, Peptostreptococcus, Pseudomonas, </italic><italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli,&#x000a0;</italic>and <italic toggle="yes">Haemophilus influenzae.</italic><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r21">[21]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Causative organisms for deep neck infections include gram-positive organisms,&#x000a0;such as&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus viridans, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and Staphylococcus aureus;</italic>&#x000a0;and gram-negative organisms, such as&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli, Klebsiella oxytoca, and Haemophilus influenzae,&#x000a0;</italic>among&#x000a0;others.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r23">[23]</xref> In studies of retropharyngeal abscesses, polymicrobial infections were identified in nearly 90% of patients. Aerobes were found in all patients, and anaerobes in more than half. Anaerobes may include <italic toggle="yes">Peptostreptococcus</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Fusobacterium</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Prevotella</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">Actinomyces.</italic>&#x000a0;Other bacteria may include <italic toggle="yes">Lactobacillus, Bacteroides, </italic>and <italic toggle="yes">Propionibacterium</italic>, among others. Some studies report cultures with an average of at least&#x000a0;5 isolates. Study results have also shown an association between biofilm and abscess development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A deep neck space abscess should&#x000a0;be considered when patients describe any or many of&#x000a0;the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe neck pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recent dental procedures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Upper respiratory infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neck or mouth trauma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trismus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Respiratory distress</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dysphagia or dysphonia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Odynophagia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Immunosuppression</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Particular red flags include neck asymmetry, neck masses, lymphadenopathy, trismus, medial displacement of the lateral pharyngeal wall and tonsil, torticollis, cranial nerve involvement, spiking fevers, and tachypnea/shortness of breath. A comprehensive physical examination should begin with vital signs and proceed from there. Particular attention should be paid to the skin and scalp examination, noting any erythema, edema, tenderness to palpation, or warmth.</p>
        <p>Oral and oropharyngeal examinations are also critical, assessing tonsillar and pharyngeal wall symmetry and noting any mucosal or periodontal lesions. A dental exam for carious, fractured, or loose teeth is required. Palpation of the tongue and floor of the mouth may reveal induration, and palpation of the parotid and submandibular glands, while observing the ducts for expressable pus, is essential. The neck examination should focus on masses or lymphadenopathy, which may be warm or painful on palpation. A cranial nerve examination should be performed to evaluate for focal deficits, and flexible fiberoptic laryngoscopy can further evaluate pharyngeal asymmetry or bulging and the airway.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Computed tomography (CT) of the neck with intravenous contrast can help confirm and localize a deep neck infection and differentiate a phlegmon from a true abscess. An orthopantomogram may help evaluate dentition, though CT may also be used to evaluate odontogenic infections. Laryngoscopy may be beneficial for assessing the presence of swelling and evaluating the airway.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;Blood work, including electrolytes, clotting profiles, blood cultures, and abscess cultures, should be obtained.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of deep neck abscesses centers on two critical priorities: airway protection, source control through adequate drainage, and broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy. Airway compromise is a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in these infections; thus, early assessment and planning are essential. Swelling, trismus, or distortion of normal anatomy may render intubation difficult, and airway loss can occur suddenly. Early consultation with otolaryngology and anesthesia is imperative, with a low threshold for securing the airway in a controlled setting. In patients with significant airway obstruction, awake fiberoptic intubation or surgical airway (tracheostomy) may be necessary. When there is concern for airway instability, prompt transfer to the operating room for simultaneous evaluation, airway management, and surgical drainage should be prioritized, as this approach can be life-saving in patients at risk of impending respiratory compromise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Definitive treatment requires surgical drainage of purulent collections, typically performed in the operating room to allow optimal exposure and exploration of all involved fascial planes. While needle aspiration under image guidance may suffice for small, well-localized abscesses, most cases demand open incision and drainage to prevent recurrence or mediastinal extension. Empiric broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics must be initiated immediately, as culture results are rarely available at presentation. Regimens should cover aerobic and anaerobic oral flora, including <italic toggle="yes">Streptococcus</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>, and anaerobes such as <italic toggle="yes">Bacteroides</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Fusobacterium</italic>. Another contemporary antibiotic coverage combination could include vancomycin, cefepime, and metronidazole.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>According to Almuqamam (2024), appropriate empiric antibiotic options include intravenous nafcillin or penicillin plus gentamicin or tobramycin, ampicillin-sulbactam, or clindamycin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r8">[8]</xref> In areas with&#x000a0;a high prevalence of methicillin-resistant&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic>, vancomycin or linezolid should be added until susceptibility results are available. Once cultures and sensitivities are available, therapy should be narrowed accordingly. Identifying and addressing the underlying source, such as early dental extraction in odontogenic infections or drainage of an infected salivary gland, is crucial for preventing recurrence and ensuring complete resolution. Supportive measures, including hydration, nutritional optimization, and pain control, complement surgical and antibiotic management and improve overall outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnoses for neck abscesses include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Epiglottis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Esophagitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gastrointestinal foreign bodies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infectious mononucleosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Kawasaki disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mediastinitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Meningitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pharyngitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tonsilitis&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trachea foreign body</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Malignancy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Goiter</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vertebral osteomyelitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Branchial cleft remnant</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>With prompt treatment, patients generally have favorable outcomes. Delays in treatment and/or additional comorbidities can result in complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The most feared complication of deep neck infections is airway compromise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r29">[29]</xref> Other complications may include internal jugular vein thrombosis, erosion into the carotid artery and rupture, mediastinitis, aspiration pneumonia, osteomyelitis, sepsis, and septic shock.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s13" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Blood and abscess cultures should be obtained, and goal-directed antibiotic therapy should be instituted as soon as results are available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Early phlegmon that has not formally coalesced into an abscess is managed medically with intravenous antibiotics and serial clinical exams. Imaging is reserved for clinical deterioration. If the patient was intubated for infection, airway edema resolution is monitored clinically until an air leak around the endotracheal tube occurs when the cuff is deflated; the patient can then be safely extubated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;If the abscess was drained surgically, a drain may be left in place. The output should be monitored daily, and the drain removed when fluid output falls below a set threshold; this threshold can vary according to institutional preference.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s14" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Otolaryngology consultation is warranted for surgical drainage if a deep neck abscess is present. If the abscess is prevertebral, neurosurgical consultation is required, as they will perform the drainage. An oral surgery consultation may be indicated for tooth extraction in cases of odontogenic abscesses.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s15" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education are essential components in reducing the incidence, severity, and recurrence of deep neck abscesses. Because many cases originate from preventable infections&#x02014;most commonly odontogenic or oropharyngeal&#x02014;effective prevention hinges on early recognition and treatment of local infections, as well as on addressing the underlying risk factors. Initial symptoms of deep neck infection may be subtle and easily mistaken for benign upper respiratory tract infections or other less serious illnesses, thereby delaying diagnosis and treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r33">[33]</xref> Patients should be counseled that early evaluation is crucial, especially when symptoms such as progressive neck pain, swelling, dysphagia, odynophagia, trismus, or fever develop. In individuals who are immunosuppressed or have significant comorbidities, such as diabetes, malignancy, or chronic steroid use, there should be a lower threshold to seek medical attention, given the potential for rapid progression and airway compromise.</p>
        <p>Good oral hygiene and routine dental care remain the cornerstone of prevention, as neglected dental caries, periapical infections, and periodontal disease are major contributors to disease. Educating patients to seek prompt evaluation for dental or pharyngeal pain, dysphagia, or swelling can prevent localized infections from extending into deep neck spaces. Clinicians should emphasize the importance of completing prescribed antibiotic courses for oropharyngeal or dental infections and of avoiding self-treatment or delaying care.</p>
        <p>In patients recovering from deep neck abscesses, education should focus on recognizing early warning signs of recurrence or complications, such as increasing neck pain, dysphagia, voice changes, trismus, or fever. Instruction in proper wound care, adherence to follow-up visits, smoking cessation, and nutritional optimization further reduce the risk of recurrence. Finally, interprofessional collaboration between clinicians, dentists, and primary care providers is critical for reinforcing consistent health maintenance and facilitating early referral for concerning or unresolved infections. These strategies collectively enhance prevention, promote early detection, and improve overall patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s16" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>The anatomy of the neck is quite&#x000a0;complex. The&#x000a0;2 principal fascial planes are the superficial cervical fascia and the deep cervical fascia. The deep neck spaces include&#x000a0;the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Parapharyngeal space (ie, lateral pharyngeal space/pharyngomaxillary space/pterygomaxillary space/pterygoid-pharyngeal space)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retropharyngeal space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prevertebral space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Danger space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Masticator space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Submandibular space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carotid space, pretracheal space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Peritonsillar space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Parotid space</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Temporal space</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25599.s17" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Management of deep neck abscesses demands a high level of clinical skill, vigilance, and interprofessional coordination to ensure optimal patient-centered outcomes and safety. Because these infections can rapidly compromise the airway or spread to critical spaces such as the mediastinum, brain, or spinal cord, early recognition and decisive management are essential. Primary care clinicians play a key role in initial detection, as most patients first present with nonspecific symptoms such as neck pain, swelling, or fever. A streamlined referral process and adherence to clinical pathways are critical to minimizing delays in diagnosis and treatment, as pediatric data indicate that adherence to guidelines significantly reduces morbidity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r34">[34]</xref> Once a deep neck infection is suspected, multidisciplinary collaboration among radiologists, surgeons, anesthesiologists, infectious disease specialists, pharmacists, nurses, and laboratory technologists is crucial for comprehensive evaluation and management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>Radiologists assist in determining the extent and location of the abscess through contrast-enhanced imaging and, in select cases, provide image-guided drainage. Otolaryngologists or thoracic surgeons perform surgical debridement or drainage, often in coordination with anesthesiologists skilled in complex airway management. Laboratory technologists and infectious disease experts guide pathogen identification and antibiotic selection, while pharmacists monitor drug levels and prevent adverse interactions. Nurses and advanced clinicians provide continuous assessment, airway monitoring, infection control, and patient and family education&#x02014;ensuring that all care decisions align with patient needs and safety. Since deep neck abscesses are considered surgical emergencies, early operative intervention has consistently been associated with improved outcomes and reduced complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25599.r36">[36]</xref> Effective communication, timely escalation of care, and clearly defined roles within the interprofessional team enhance efficiency, reduce error, and optimize short- and long-term patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
